    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d151-a1">

                <pb facs="0294" n="200" sameAs="frus:frus1896/pg200"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Robertson to
                            Burgomaster Versmann.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Consulate of the United States of America</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Hamburg</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1894-01-12"><hi rendition="#italic">January
                                12, 1894</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Replying to the valued favor of your
                    magnificence of the 10th instant, I have the honor to inform you that in July or
                    August last this consulate succeeded in communicating with one Stanislas de <pb
                        facs="0295" n="201" xml:id="pg201"/> Kruszeroski, in Zbrucz, a distant
                    relative of Vladimir von Suminski, who, under date of 5/17 of August last, wrote
                    that he had already expended money to bring Suminski from the United States to
                    Russia and that he could do nothing more in his behalf.</p>
                <p>He mentioned also that the only living relatives of Suminski were two
                    hall-brothers of his father, who were not bound to him and who besides had not
                    the means to assist him.</p>
                <p>I communicated these facts to my Government, and its reply inclines me to the
                    belief that there is no fund from which means could be provided for bringing
                    Suminski to the United States at the expense of my country.</p>
                <p>The fact that he is an alien would not exclude him from institutions established
                    in the United States in behalf of the sick or of paupers. We provide for such
                    aliens there, and expect that a foreign government will give our citizens the
                    same treatment.</p>
                <p>I have taken the liberty of venturing these last suggestions to your
                    magnificence, and trust that the information contained herein may be of
                    service.</p>
                <p>With renewed assurances, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. Henry Robertson</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">United States Consul.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
